# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clal-drawing-sysvars-silent-out-of-dialect — two clautolisp-only sysvars answer under every dialect without a warning
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: pjb-decision
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-27

* Observed

Measured on the built binary while verifying
=autolisp-globals-writability-triage= (now closed). Probing by EXISTENCE — a
name that does not exist answers =nil=, anything else answers a value:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp-sbcl -norc -q --dialect autocad -l probe.lsp
dialect=AUTOCAD | SAVEFORMAT=1 | TEMPLATEPATH= | CLAL-template= | bogus=nil
#+end_example

So under =--dialect autocad=:

- =CLAUTOLISPNEWDRAWINGTEMPLATE= exists and answers (=""=), and so does
  =CLAUTOLISPDEFAULTDRAWINGFORMAT=;
- nothing is written to =*error-output*= — the read is entirely silent
  (checked with stdout discarded, stderr alone).

Both are declared clautolisp-only. =cador/source/sysvars.lisp=:

#+begin_quote
=*clautolisp-extension-sysvar-names*= — "The clautolisp-specific system
variables with *no vendor counterpart*"
#+end_quote

* The question, and why it is pjb's rather than a defect to fix

AGENTS.md § /Dialect Divergence and Warnings/, case 1: an *Extension* —
present in some implementations only — "clautolisp runs it; warn under any
dialect that isn't the owner (=lax= silent)". Read literally, these two should
warn under =strict=, =--autocad= and =--bricscad=, and they do not.

But the tree shows the gate is *deliberately mixed*, decided name by name.
=autolisp-builtins-core/source/secureload.lisp= gates three clautolisp-only
sysvars behind =(when clautolisp-p ...)= — =CLAUTOLISPSUPPORTFILESEARCHPATH=,
=CLAUTOLISPIMPLICITLYTRUSTEDFOLDERPATHS=, =CLAUTOLISPDROP= — and immediately
after, a *pjb ruling* moves another one OUT of that gate, with the reason
recorded:

#+begin_quote
Case-insensitive path resolution: EVERY dialect but =--strict= (pjb's ruling,
case-insensitive-paths-ignored-in-cad-dialects). It was clautolisp-only, which
put it exactly where it was least needed: a =--bricscad= / =--autocad= run is
how a corpus WRITTEN FOR WINDOWS is exercised, and that is the corpus whose
path spellings nobody ever checked.
#+end_quote

That reasoning may well apply to these two as well: a =--bricscad= run is
exactly when you want to choose the template and the save format, and a sysvar
that warns every time it is read would be noise on the path where it is most
useful. So this is not obviously a bug — it is a choice with a precedent on
both sides, and the point of this ticket is that *which choice it is should be
written down* instead of re-derived by the next reader.

* Options

1. *Warn, deduped.* Emit the extension warning on first access per sysvar per
   session, silent under =:clautolisp= and =:lax=. Precedent for the shape:
   =emit-terpri-file-extension-warning=, and the per-path/session dedup of the
   =[path-dotdot]= warning.
2. *Exempt deliberately*, like the case-insensitive sysvar, and record the
   exemption in the =*** clautolisp= note of each sysvar's spec page so the
   silence is documented rather than accidental.
3. *Gate them to =:clautolisp== like the three trust sysvars — the strictest
   reading, and the one most likely to break a =--bricscad= invocation that
   currently works.

Option 2 is the cheapest if the current behaviour is what pjb wants; option 1
is what a literal reading of AGENTS.md asks for. Either way the outcome is one
paragraph of documentation, which is why this is P3 and not larger.

* Not done here, deliberately

No code change. Adding a warning is user-visible behaviour on a path that a
=--bricscad= invocation may rely on, and the tree shows this exact question has
already been decided *both ways* by the owner for different sysvars. Guessing
which way it goes for these two is how a deliberate exemption gets silently
reversed.

* Note

Filed per AGENTS.md's rule for something found while doing something else. The
measurement detail is worth keeping: a sysvar value of =""= does NOT distinguish
"absent" from "present and empty", so a probe must test existence (=nil= or
not). The first version of that probe read values and showed the same thing
under three dialects while proving nothing.
